#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 660.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172890} :activity/id #uuid "b43f46e4-16a6-5109-897b-7d61b6455c74" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048493 :topic/id #uuid "a45cb440-1b11-5eea-ac7d-09ef4b31794b" :topic/math-academy-id 3335} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172882 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172025 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171831 :question/id #uuid "2a7b5d23-3dc7-57f8-98ed-72b1a45e787a" :question/math-academy-id "e-8157"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a6598e4e-7ea6-56ce-abef-95e64d010e96" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048849} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049113} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050021}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172611} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172614} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272153} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357616} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357617} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357620} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357621} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492008}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Parts of an Orthogonal Diagonalization"} :step/id #uuid "a2aa8190-261c-5faa-a399-c992ada54b4e" :step/math-academy-id 30552 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172884}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172883 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172026 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171832 :question/id #uuid "7d2dc7ea-947d-5cda-8c95-c28281ecd792" :question/math-academy-id "e-8158"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4754d4e6-b2d0-5efb-ac92-c004c70004df" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172613} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172616} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272156} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357636} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357637} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186364509} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186364516} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404612} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404619} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492002}] :knowledge-point/title "Orthogonally Diagonalizing a 2x2 Symmetric Matrix"} :step/id #uuid "af3ae02e-e0d1-5714-a823-36990c3416dc" :step/math-academy-id 30553} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172884 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172027 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171833 :question/id #uuid "fd780d94-dd75-5a57-b34d-4bbaffcb95ce" :question/math-academy-id "e-8164"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "31ae5cdc-ae3f-5497-88d8-f02a7263340d" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172612} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172615} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272154} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357630} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357631} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186364502} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186394880} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186394887} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186394894} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492025} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492035}] :knowledge-point/title "Orthogonally Diagonalizing a 2x2 Symmetric Matrix Given Part of the Diagonalization"} :step/id #uuid "136b060b-1871-5958-8dc0-d7310998c040" :step/math-academy-id 30578 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172883}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172890 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173154 :tutorial/content "A square matrix $A$ is **orthogonally diagonalizable** if there exists an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that\n\n$$\nA=PDP^T,\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a diagonal matrix. Note that this is equivalent to the equation\n\n$$\nD = P^TAP.\n$$\n\nSo, to orthogonally diagonalize a matrix $A,$ we need to find a diagonal matrix $D$ and an orthogonal matrix $P$ that satisfies the above equations.\n\nA helpful theorem allows us to determine whether a real matrix is orthogonally diagonalizable. It states the following:\n\n*A matrix $A$ with real entries is orthogonally diagonalizable if and only if it is symmetric.*\n\nConsequently, *every* symmetric matrix is orthogonally diagonalizable!\n\nFor example, the symmetric matrix \n\n![](images/b3/b3970eb885720ce2aa4394918c6c5f9f343e26aa60039df219abf56398f096db.svg)\n\nhas the orthogonal diagonalization\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={{\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} & -\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{P}{{\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -6\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{D}{{{\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} & -\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{bmatrix}}^{T}}_{⏟}}_{{P}^{T}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $P$ is orthogonal, and $D$ is diagonal. As usual, the entries on the main diagonal of $D$ are the eigenvalues of $A.$ Also, the columns of $P$ are *orthonormal* eigenvectors corresponding to the eigenvalues of $A.$\n\nThe procedure of the orthogonal diagonalization of a $2 \\times 2$ real symmetric matrix $A$ is almost the same as the diagonalization of a matrix:\n\n- **Step 1**: Find the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$.\n\n- **Step 2**: Find a basis of  $\\mathbb{R}^2$ consisting of *unit orthogonal* eigenvectors of $A.$\n\n- **Step 3**: Construct the matrices $D$ and $P.$\n\nIn the case of symmetric matrices, the two eigenvectors we compute in step 2 will always be orthogonal, provided that they correspond to different eigenvalues. However, we must also ensure that we *normalize* those eigenvectors." :tutorial/id #uuid "c729ab04-2eef-5b7c-a28e-3d0f55428c54" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5006 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "337f89b5-9c83-5bda-ac19-7d80b551f82e" :step/math-academy-id 31262 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172882}}] :activity/title "Diagonalization of 2x2 Symmetric Matrices" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186172166}]}
